# Line endings, and why they are not a style preference here.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default, and it would check these files
# out with CRLF. That breaks `check:vendor`, which verifies `vendor/pi` against
# a sha256 manifest: a line-ending conversion changes every hash in it, so the
# guard would fail on a fresh clone while passing on the machine that produced
# it — the worst kind of failure, because it only shows up for other people.
#
# Forcing LF in the working tree makes the bytes identical on every platform,
# which is the assumption the manifest is built on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the one format that genuinely requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.exe binary
*.7z binary
*.zip binary
